# Native UIA lifetime: long offscreen crossover driver

This is an unexecuted external diagnostic driver for a matched 1,000-frame-per-round
x64 Debug and Release A-B-B-A run. It follows the retained short crossover in
`../native-uia-final-perf-confirmation-20260928/crossover/`; those raw reports,
logs, and comparison sidecars remain in place and are hashed before and after
the long run. The new destination is
`../native-uia-long-crossover-20260930/`, which must not already exist.

After the machine is quiet, run from PowerShell 7:

```powershell
py -3 C:/RedSalamander.Perf/evidence/i26-ui/native-uia-long-driver-20260930/run_long_crossover.py
```

The driver checks the exact A/B commits, clean compiled and test inputs, matching
original benchmark header bytes, restored x64 dependencies, Visual Studio via
`vswhere -prerelease`, the comparator-only edit, and all original short receipts.
It freezes the original header bytes under the new output directory, applies the
same exact header edit to both checkouts, and rebuilds both x64 configurations
with each checkout's original `build.ps1 -Rebuild`. The two long builds are
completed before measurement. It then measures A1, B1, B2, A2 serially in each
configuration through the external `performance_long.ps1`, which uses the
original `performance.ps1` receipt metadata and the original `--benchmark`
entry point. The header edit adjusts only the sample count, array size, FPS
divisor and p50/p95 indices. `benchmark-1000.patch` shows the intended change.

The stock 40-frame comparator remains untouched in both repositories. The
external `compare_performance_long.py` changes only the expected frame-count
validation to 1,000; all metric definitions and investigation bands remain
unchanged. The driver records a narrowly checked stock-comparator
`invalid-evidence` result against each long A1 receipt; its error must be
exactly the expected forty-frame rejection. It compares all four A/B
cross-pairings plus A1/A2 and B1/B2 repeats
for each configuration. An `advice-required` result is retained as evidence and
does not mean the driver failed; `invalid-evidence`, fixture mismatches and
failed measurements stop the run. The short 40-frame baseline must not be
compared to the long fixture because the workload count and fixture hash differ.

The `finally` path writes back the exact frozen header bytes and runs
`build.ps1 -Rebuild` for both configurations of every patched checkout. It
records build and run logs, exit codes, hashes, comparison statuses, and
restoration status in `driver-result.json`. Full compiled/test input hashes are
checked before mutation, around builds and runs, and after restoration; the
final receipt records rebuilt executable hashes. If a process is forcibly killed
before `finally` completes, the original bytes are in
`native-uia-long-crossover-20260930/original-headers/`; restore each matching
header, verify its SHA-256 against `driver-result.json`, and rebuild both x64
configurations in each checkout before using those binaries. Never overwrite a
concurrent header edit; the driver detects that conflict and leaves its backup.

The edited benchmark header is under `Tests`, so `benchmarkSha256` and its
`benchmarkInputs` entry change together in A and B. The library
`sourceFingerprint` from `performance.ps1` hashes `src`, `include` and `Build`
inputs and therefore does not change solely from this header edit. Receipts
still report the checkout dirty while the long fixture is installed.

This fixture measures completed offscreen WARP rendering, not the WindowHost UIA
snapshot/token path or presented FPS. Its long run can clarify the short-run
Release clean timing direction and process resource variation, but it cannot
qualify native UIA cost or ARM64. Interpret all individual flags and same-binary
variation before making a regression decision.
